Does Democracy Still Need the Many? Technology, Enclosure and the Evolution of the Citizen

By Claudette M Raynor

Abstract
This essay traces the parallel histories of democracy and technology to show how the rise and fall of democratic self-government has tracked a single recurring question: whether a society's dominant technologies make ordinary people more necessary or less. Drawing on work in the history of information, labor, political economy and surveillance, including Gleick, Lucassen, Mumford, Polanyi, Braverman, Scott, Foucault and Zuboff, it identifies a repeating pattern I call enclosure: a historical moment in which something people once held in common, or used freely in ordinary life, is captured by trade and government and turned into a commodity that can be owned and controlled. Each major technological shift first opens something to the many, spreading power and allowing democracy to rise, and is then followed by an enclosure that concentrates power and pushes democracy back. Four enclosures mark this path, each producing a distinct form of citizen. The enclosure of mind and labor produced the biological citizen. The enclosure of property and goods produced the natural rights citizen. The enclosure of labor and data produced the surveillance citizen. And the enclosure of knowledge, now extending to design, engineering and the processes of life itself, is producing the algorithmic citizen. The essay argues that artificial intelligence marks a break in this sequence, because it may allow systems of production, governance and even creation to function without needing the many at all, removing the leverage that has historically compelled power to be shared.

This essay serves as an introduction to a larger work in progress, which more fully documents the impact of artificial intelligence on the traditional ideals of life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness, from the earliest human societies forward. The framework of the four citizens and their enclosures emerged from that research and forms its foundation.

Introduction 

When we look at the history of democracy and technology it often moves so interconnectedly that it is hard to establish if one is the cause for the effect on the other or vice versa. When we created fire we invented cooking which made us move into larger groups which made the greek concept the polis become more necessary as group size gradually increased over time. However, these groups were not growing anywhere near as fast as that neat narrative suggests; as Lucassen points out in The Story of Work, the jump from cooking around a fire to organized civic bodies like the polis took hundreds of thousands of years, meaning tech doesn't automatically force larger political structures into existence—it sits dormant until changes in work, labor, and surplus force us to reorganize.

Democracy over the course of history has gone through several periods of rise and fall for different reasons, but what is more interesting is how those rises and falls correspond to the evolution of technology. While democracy tends to rise when power is spread out across many people and tends to fall when power gets concentrated or when a crisis makes people willing to trade freedom for order, technology plays a critical role in the concentration of power—to such an extent that it becomes nearly impossible to extricate which came first: technology or democracy?

A quick history of democracy

While there is no official number for how many times democracy has risen and fallen throughout humanity’s history, we do know that it has never been stable and we can at least identify several key turning points. 
Ancient Athens was perhaps the marked rise of democracy with its set up around 508 BC, and other Greek cities followed. Athens itself flipped back and forth even within that period, with oligarchic takeovers in 411 and 404 BC, before Macedon ended its democracy for good in 322 BC. Rome had a republic with some popular elements, though it was never a full democracy, and that ended when Augustus became emperor in 27 BC, so the ancient wave had actually mostly faded just before the time of Christ, not after.
After that came a long stretch of more than a thousand years when empires and monarchies dominated. Self-government never fully disappeared, though; it survived in pockets. Iceland's Althing assembly dates to 930. Medieval Italian city-republics like Venice and Florence governed themselves, as did the Swiss cantons with their open-air assemblies. Novgorod had a town assembly, and the Haudenosaunee Confederacy in North America ran on a council system. England's Parliament grew slowly over the same centuries.
For the modern era, political scientist Samuel Huntington described democracy as waves. A first long wave ran from the 1820s to the 1920s, as the US, Britain and much of Europe widened voting rights. A reverse wave followed in the 1920s and 30s, when fascist and authoritarian regimes took over in Italy, Germany, Spain and elsewhere. The second wave came after World War II, with occupied countries rebuilt as democracies and many former colonies gaining independence. A second reverse wave, roughly 1960 to the mid-1970s, saw military coups across Latin America, Greece, Africa and parts of Asia. The third wave began with Portugal's revolution in 1974 and swept through Spain, Latin America, East Asia, and then Eastern Europe after the Berlin Wall fell in 1989.
Many researchers think a third reverse wave has been underway since the mid-2000s. Groups like Freedom House and V-Dem have recorded more countries losing democratic ground than gaining it each year. How deep or lasting that slide is remains debated, and the picture varies a lot from country to country.
Put together, that's about four forward surges (ancient Greece, then three modern waves) and three or four retreats (the ancient collapse, two modern reverse waves, and possibly the current one). If you count individual countries, the number explodes. France alone has gone through five republics, two empires and several monarchies since 1789.

So why the rise and fall? One of the oldest drivers is the hoplite question (referring to the heavily armored citizen-soldiers who formed the backbone of ancient Greek armies): whoever the state needs to fight its wars can demand a say. Greek cities needed their middle-class infantry, Athens needed its poor rowers, and in the modern era mass conscription armies played a similar role. After both world wars, governments that had asked millions of men and women to sacrifice found it hard to keep denying them the vote. Economic change works the same way. When wealth shifts from a few landowners to a broad class of merchants, artisans and later industrial workers, those people gain the leverage to insist on representation. The economists Daron Acemoglu and James Robinson argue that elites often extended voting rights not out of generosity but because the threat of revolt made it the cheaper option. Information matters too: the printing press, rising literacy, newspapers and later mass media all made it harder for rulers to monopolize what people knew. And democracy spreads by example. When neighboring countries democratize, the idea starts to seem possible, which is part of why the modern waves came in clusters.

On the fall side, the causes are close to mirror images. Conquest ended many democracies outright, the way Macedon ended Athenian self-rule. Growth itself can break institutions. Rome's republican system was built to run a city, and as it became an empire, generals like Marius, Sulla, and Caesar commanded armies loyal to them personally rather than to the state, and that power eventually overwhelmed the republic. Economic disaster is one of the strongest predictors of breakdown. The Great Depression and Germany's hyperinflation helped make fascism attractive to voters who wanted someone to restore order. Deep polarization does similar damage. When rival sides come to see each other as enemies rather than opponents, they start to tolerate rule-breaking by their own side. Steven Levitsky and Daniel Ziblatt, in How Democracies Die, argue that democracies depend on unwritten norms like mutual tolerance and restraint, and that once those erode, formal rules alone don't hold. Outside powers have also played a role. During the Cold War, both superpowers backed coups and friendly dictators when it suited them.
One modern shift is in how democracies end. In the 20th century it was often a military coup with tanks in the street. More recently, researchers describe a slower process, sometimes called executive aggrandizement, in which elected leaders gradually weaken courts, the press and election rules while keeping the outward forms of democracy in place. That's a large part of why scholars debate how serious the current slide is: it is harder to measure than a coup.
The ancient Greeks had their own theory of all this. The historian Polybius described anacyclosis, a cycle in which monarchy decays into tyranny, is overthrown by aristocracy, which decays into oligarchy, which gives way to democracy, which decays into mob rule, which invites a strongman, and around it goes again. Modern historians don't think history runs on a fixed loop like that. Still, his core point holds up: every system carries the conditions that can undo it, and democracy lasts only as long as enough people keep power spread out and keep choosing to share it.
Mapping technology to the history of democracy 

Technology maps surprisingly well onto democracy’s rise and fall as long as you look at whether a technology spreads power out or concentrates it. Technology by itself isn't democratic or authoritarian. What matters is who it makes necessary, and who it makes powerful. 

One of the greatest ways to trace this power is through weapons. Lewis Mumford spent much of his career showing how tightly technology and war are bound together, each shaping the other. In Technics and Civilization (1934) he argued that war was one of the great engines of mechanization. Armies drove the growth of mining and metallurgy, pushed standardized parts and uniforms, and drilled soldiers to move as interchangeable units, so the army became a model for the factory that followed. Thirty years later, in his essay "Authoritarian and Democratic Technics" (1964), he distinguished two kinds of technology. Democratic technics are small in scale, centered on the person, and widely held. Authoritarian technics are large, centrally controlled, and enormously powerful, and he traced them back to the first kingdoms that could organize armies and labor gangs into a single human machine. For Mumford, the kind of technology a society relies on goes a long way toward deciding who holds power in it.

George Orwell made a similar argument in a 1945 essay written just after Hiroshima: eras of cheap, simple weapons tend to favor ordinary people, and eras of expensive, complex weapons tend to favor whoever can afford them. Ancient Greek democracy rose with iron and bronze working cheap enough that a middle-class farmer could buy his own armor, and with the trireme, which needed thousands of poor rowers. In the medieval period the advantage swung to the mounted, armored knight. The historian Lynn White argued, in a thesis that's still debated, that the stirrup made heavy cavalry dominant, and a knight's horse and armor cost a fortune. Power concentrated in a small warrior aristocracy, and feudalism followed. Gunpowder ended the knight. At first, though, expensive cannons favored kings who could pay for them, which helped centralized monarchies. Then the cheap musket arrived, and suddenly any trained peasant could kill a nobleman. The French Revolution's mass citizen armies grew out of that, and the political claims of those soldiers fed the first modern democratic wave. In the 20th century, machine guns, tanks and aircraft began shrinking the number of people needed to hold power by force. Nuclear weapons pushed that much further, and drones and AI may push it further still. If a state no longer needs millions of cooperating citizens to fight, it loses one of the oldest pressures toward sharing power.

Information technology follows a similar arc, but with a twist. The Greek alphabet was simple enough for ordinary citizens to learn, and Athens carved its laws in public stone. In the medieval centuries, writing was largely controlled by the church and the courts, since manuscripts were rare and costly. The printing press after 1450 broke that monopoly. It fueled the Reformation, the Dutch Republic, the English Civil War pamphlet wars, and later the American Revolution, where Thomas Paine's Common Sense sold in enormous numbers. Newspapers and the telegraph helped build mass politics in the 1800s. Then radio arrived, and it cut both ways at once. Roosevelt used it for his fireside chats, while the Nazi regime mass-produced cheap radio sets so that a single voice could reach every home. The reverse wave of the 1920s and 30s was partly a radio-age phenomenon. Later, during the third wave, smaller and harder-to-control tools helped topple regimes: fax machines, photocopied underground writing in the Soviet bloc, cassette tapes of sermons smuggled into Iran in 1979, satellite television crossing borders.

That twist is a recurring pattern. A new communication technology usually goes through a disruptive phase first, when it slips past existing gatekeepers and destabilizes whoever holds authority, whether that's a democracy or a dictatorship. Then comes a consolidation phase, when states and large organizations learn to control it, censor it, or use it themselves. The internet is following that path. In the early 2010s, during the Arab Spring, social media looked like a liberation technology. Within a decade, the story had shifted toward surveillance, disinformation, algorithmic polarization, and the concentration of attention in a few platforms. That shift lines up closely with the period researchers call the current democratic recession.
Economic technology fits in too. The industrial revolution created a large working class and a larger middle class concentrated in cities, where they could organize, strike and vote, and that pressure drove the expansion of suffrage in the first modern wave. But industrialization also produced giant concentrated enterprises and the bureaucratic machinery that totalitarian states later used.
Laid side by side, the timeline of this evolution technology roughly matches the rise and fall of democracy. Ancient democracy coincided with the alphabet, cheap iron and citizen-crewed fleets. The long gap coincided with the armored knight and the hand-copied manuscript. The modern waves followed the printing press, the musket and industrialization. The reverse waves line up with radio, mechanized warfare and later Cold War military technology. The current slide coincides with platform media and digital surveillance. Historians are cautious about pure technological determinism, since culture, religion, economics and individual choices all matter enormously. Still, the most useful question to ask of any new technology is whether it makes more people necessary or fewer, and whether it makes it easier or harder for a few hands to hold the levers.
The question of AI
If modern business and democracy both rely on producing and selling something that makes more people necessary then how does AI answer this question. Is AI making more people necessary? 
Perhaps Dario Amodei comes the closest when in his January 2026 essay The Adolescence of Technology, he makes the same argument this conversation has been tracing from the hoplites forward. He writes that current autocracies are limited in how repressive they can be because they need humans to carry out their orders, and humans often have limits on how inhumane they're willing to be. This applies the "who does the state need" question directly to AI: if machines can do the enforcing, that old check disappears. He applies the same logic to the economy. He argues the old pattern of displaced workers retraining for new jobs may not hold when AI can outperform humans at almost everything, which could produce a new underclass, mass unemployment, and an even more extreme concentration of wealth and power that threatens the foundations of democracy. He had already said publicly in 2025 that AI could wipe out a large share of entry-level white-collar jobs within a few years.
Sam Altman has approached it from the economic side. In his 2021 essay Moore's Law for Everything, he argued that AI would drive the value of labor down while the value of capital rose, and proposed taxing companies and land to spread the gains to everyone. He has since floated ideas like "universal basic compute." So he has asked a version of the question, but his answer leans toward redistribution: if people become less necessary as workers, give them a share of the output instead. Critics point out that this doesn't restore the leverage people had when they were needed. A share handed to you can be taken away in a way that your labor's necessity can't.
Geoffrey Hinton, a founding father of AI,  is the bluntest. Since leaving Google he has said repeatedly that in the current economic system, AI will let the wealthy replace workers, producing mass unemployment and huge profits, making a few people much richer and most people poorer. He frames that as a problem of capitalism rather than of the technology itself, and he has backed universal basic income while doubting it solves the loss of dignity and purpose that comes with not being needed.
Outside those three, the sharpest version of the idea is an essay called The Intelligence Curse by Luke Drago and Rudolf Laine from 2025. It borrows from the "resource curse," where oil-rich states often become less democratic because rulers draw wealth from the ground instead of from taxpaying citizens, so they stop needing to invest in their people. The essay argues AI could do the same thing to every economy at once.
Many of the longest-lived companies built ecosystems that made huge numbers of people more necessary: Microsoft with the developers who wrote Windows software, Apple with App Store developers, Shopify with independent merchants, YouTube with creators. Each grew by making itself the platform others built livelihoods on, and those people in turn became loyal to it and dependent on its success. Henry Ford's old logic points the same way: workers are also customers, so an economy that makes people unnecessary eventually erodes its own market.
However, plenty of companies have also succeeded by making people less necessary, through automation and cutting labor costs, but history shows a recurring twist there. Making one task unnecessary often made people more necessary somewhere else. ATMs, for example, were expected to eliminate bank tellers, but for decades banks opened more branches and teller jobs actually grew, with the role shifting toward customer service. The open question with AI is whether that twist still works when the technology can potentially step into the new roles too. Anthropic has started measuring this directly through its Economic Index, which tracks whether people use Claude to automate tasks away or to augment their own work. Which way that number moves over the next decade may say a lot about which side of your question the industry ends up on.
The Evolution of the Citizen and the nature of historical enclosures
Technology and democracy don't simply run alongside each other, but they keep reshaping the conditions the other depends on. Plenty of books trace the history of technology, history of information, history of democracy, history of work, or history of privacy, but few step back to look at how these histories fit together and push on one another. Seen side by side, they reveal a recurring pattern that I call enclosure.
An enclosure is a point in history where something people once held in common, or used freely as part of ordinary life, is captured by trade and government and turned into a commodity that can be owned and controlled. The term comes from the English enclosures, when common land that villagers had farmed and grazed for centuries was fenced off into private property, and Marx treated that fencing-off as one of the founding acts of capitalism. But land was only one of the things to be enclosed. Each major turn in technology has opened something new to the many, and an enclosure has followed, capturing it for the few. Each enclosure has also changed the nature of the democratic citizen: what the citizen is, what the citizen has to offer, and what the system needs from them. Over time, the citizen has come to be defined less by membership in a political community and more by what can be taken from them and traded. This moves toward the condition Marx described when he wrote that under capitalism human labor itself becomes a commodity, bought and sold like any other good. Followed to its end, the process risks dissolving the citizen into the commodity.
Four enclosures mark this path, and each produced its own kind of citizen. The enclosure of mind and labor produced the biological citizen. The enclosure of property and goods produced the natural rights of citizens. The enclosure of labor and data produced the surveillance citizen. And the enclosure of knowledge is producing the algorithmic citizen.
The Biological Citizen: The Enclosure of Mind and Labor (800 BCE – 1500/1600)
The first enclosure begins with the alphabetic revolution and the shift from oral to written culture and the introduction of writing. Before writing, knowledge lived in people. Oral cultures were not poor in information; they built sophisticated ways to carry it. The talking drums of sub-Saharan Africa, for example, sent messages across miles of forest by imitating the tones of speech and wrapping each message in long, repeated phrases so that its meaning survived the distance (Gleick, The Information, ch. 1). In such cultures, knowledge lived in memory, in formula and performance, and in the community that heard it and passed it on. It was held in common because it had nowhere else to live.
Writing not only changed where knowledge lived, but how we build upon existing knowledge. Once it could be written down, it no longer had to be remembered, but could be looked up and built upon in a way that was much harder to do with oral tradition or shared speech. In Plato's Phaedrus, the Egyptian king Thamus warns the inventor of letters that writing will bring forgetfulness rather than wisdom, because people will come to trust external marks instead of the memory within. That philosophical warning captures a deeper structural shift: once knowledge could be fixed in an object, it no longer needed a living mind to carry it. And unlike an ephemeral memory, an object can be owned, stored, copied, withheld, and controlled—marking the very first enclosure of human cognition.
By moving memory out of people and into objects, writing didn't just give the mind a new tool; it gave thinking a completely new form. Literacy made possible the abstract, categorical reasoning that later generations took for granted, such as definitions, classifications, and formal logic, with Aristotle's syllogism among its earliest products. When the psychologist Alexander Luria studied unschooled peasants in Central Asia in the 1930s, he found that they reasoned from lived experience rather than abstract premises, refusing logic puzzles about places they had never seen (Ong; Havelock; Luria; as discussed in Gleick, ch. 2). Because this new form of thought had to be taught, it captured the mind within written language, creating a specialized mode of literacy that only the trained could enter and gatekeep. 
For a time, ancient Greece pushed the other way. The Greek alphabet was simple enough for ordinary citizens to learn, and Athens carved its laws in public stone where anyone could read them. Knowledge that had lived in memory and speech became something many people could hold. That opening came with a narrow definition of who belonged. Pericles' citizenship law of 451 BCE limited Athenian citizenship to people with two Athenian parents, so citizenship was literally a matter of blood (jus sanguinis). Even at its height, Athenian democracy excluded women and rested on enslaved labor. The citizen was defined by the body: by birth, and by the ability to fight and row. Democracy rose because the city needed those bodies. It needed middle-class hoplites for its phalanx and poor rowers for its fleet.
The enclosure followed in two directions, and they were two sides of the same division. The mind was enclosed first from the outside, as writing settled into the hands of scribes, clergy, and courts through the medieval centuries. Manuscripts were rare and costly, the language of learning was Latin rather than the vernacular spoken by ordinary people, and literacy became the exclusive property of institutions rather than a shared human tool. Whoever held the books held the record of what was law, what was true, and what had happened.
Writing changed the very nature of logic and how meaning was understood. Once words were fixed on the page, meaning seemed to live in the text itself, stable and separate from the people who spoke it, rather than being negotiated between people in conversation. Thought began to move along the rigid paths writing laid down: definition, category, premise, and conclusion. The written word didn't just record what people thought; it set the boundaries for how they could think, and whoever controlled the writing controlled that frame.
In the Phaedrus, Socrates objects that a written text cannot answer back. Question it, and it gives the exact same reply every time; it cannot explain itself, defend itself, or adapt to the person reading it. While living dialogue lets thought move freely, testing and reshaping itself on the fly, writing fixes thought in stone and demands that the reader follow its tracks. Through this shift, the mind was captured within language, turning human cognition itself into something that could be shaped, gated, and owned.
The enclosure of labor has roots older than the alphabet, reaching back to the shift from foraging to farming. For most of human history, people lived in small groups where work was organized by reciprocity: food was gathered and shared within the household and the band, and no one could live for long on the labor of others. Agriculture, beginning around ten thousand years ago, changed that. Farming produced a surplus, a store of grain that could be kept, counted and taken, and for the first time some people could live off the work of others. As villages grew into towns and cities, the old reciprocal labor of the household was joined by tributary labor, in which temples and palaces collected grain and work from the many and handed rations back. In time came both wage labor and unfree labor in the form of slavery (Lucassen, The Story of Work).
Writing was born to manage that surplus, which tied the enclosure of the mind to the enclosure of labor from the very beginning. The earliest writing, in the Mesopotamian city of Uruk around 3200 BCE, was not poetry or law but accounting: clay tablets recording grain stores, rations, debts and the labor each person owed. The first states were grain states, built on crops that could be seen, measured and taxed, and writing let rulers count their people, assess what they owed, and call them up for work and war (Scott, Against the Grain). Government and writing grew up together. The written record defined what each person owed, and whoever kept the record held power over everyone recorded in it.
Ancient Greece loosened this hold for a time. Coinage, first struck in Lydia around 600 BCE, spread through the Greek world, and with it came wage labor alongside the older forms. Athens paid its rowers in coin, and under Pericles it began paying citizens for jury service, so that even the poor could afford to take part in governing. Labor and citizenship were bound together: the work the city needed from its people gave them a claim on the city. But this opening rested on slavery, and enslaved people did much of the work that freed citizens' time for politics.
After Rome fell, the opening closed. In western Europe, coinage and wage labor contracted, and work fell back into unfree forms (Lucassen). Serfdom bound peasants to land they worked but did not own, and their obligations of labor, rent and produce were fixed in the customs of the manor and increasingly in writing. In 1086, William the Conqueror ordered the Domesday Book, a survey of every manor in England, recording its land, its ploughs, its animals and the people who worked it, so the crown would know exactly what each place owed. The English came to call it "Domesday," after the Day of Judgment, because there was no appeal from what it recorded. Writing could occasionally bind rulers too, as the barons showed by forcing King John to put his promises in writing in the Magna Carta of 1215, but its protections reached only a narrow class of free men. The literate few kept the record and set the frame of thought, the unlettered many did the work, and neither group could cross into the other's ground. Once power moved to armored cavalry and hand-copied knowledge, rulers no longer needed the many, and democracy disappeared for more than a thousand years, surviving only in scattered pockets.
The Natural Rights Citizen: The Enclosure of Property and Goods (1600 – 1900)
The second enclosure begins with a reopening. When the printing press spread across Europe after 1450, it broke the monopoly that scribes, clergy and courts had held over written knowledge. Bibles appeared in the languages people actually spoke, pamphlets moved faster than authorities could suppress them, and literacy spread beyond the institutions that had guarded it. The enclosure of the mind did not end, but it loosened, and out of that loosening came a new way of thinking about the person. Where the first enclosure captured knowledge and labor, the second captured the material world: the land, the things made from it, time itself, and finally ideas, all recast as property.
The road back from monarchy to democracy ran through the cost of war. Gunpowder had ended the armored knight, but cannon, fortresses and standing armies were enormously expensive, and kings could no longer pay for them from their own estates. They had to ask for money from the people who held the wealth: landowners, merchants and townspeople, gathered in parliaments and assemblies. Each request opened a bargain. Those whose money the crown needed began to demand a say in how it was raised and spent. In England, Charles I's attempts to tax without Parliament's consent led to civil war in the 1640s, and the printing press carried the argument to the public through a flood of pamphlets. In 1647, at the Putney Debates, soldiers of the parliamentary army argued over who should vote. The Leveller Thomas Rainsborough insisted that "the poorest he that is in England hath a life to live, as the greatest he," and so should have a voice in the government he lived under. His opponent Henry Ireton answered that the vote belonged only to those with a permanent, fixed stake in the country, meaning those who owned property. Ireton's side won, and that answer would define the citizen for the next two centuries. Charles I was executed in 1649. After the monarchy's restoration and a second crisis, the Glorious Revolution of 1688 and the Bill of Rights of 1689 placed the crown under Parliament for good. The Dutch had already shown it could be done, having thrown off Spanish rule to form a republic in the late 1500s.
The rights philosophy of 1604 to 1750 gave these struggles their language. Hugo Grotius argued in 1609 that the seas were common to all and could not be owned, a case for the commons made at the very moment the commons on land were about to disappear. Thomas Hobbes, writing in 1651 in the wake of civil war, argued that people give up some freedom to a strong central authority in exchange for security. John Locke answered in 1689 with a different foundation: people are born with natural rights, and government exists to protect them. Chief among those rights was property, which a person acquired by mixing their labor with land. The idea of individual rights widened who could claim a place in political life, but the right at its center was the right to own. Locke's reasoning also had a darker use. Land that did not appear to be worked in the European way could be declared unowned, and his argument helped justify the seizure of Indigenous land in the Americas.
The American colonists' demand for "no taxation without representation" was the same bargain England's Parliament had fought for, now turned against Parliament itself, and it led to independence in 1776. In France, the crown went bankrupt in part from the cost of its wars, including its support for the American Revolution. To raise money, Louis XVI summoned the Estates-General in 1789 for the first time in 175 years, and within weeks the assembly had declared itself the voice of the nation. The French Revolution swept away absolute monarchy and proclaimed the rights of man. In both revolutions, the old pattern held: rulers who needed the many had to answer to them.
At home, the word "enclosure" became literal. England's enclosure acts, which ran heaviest from about 1750 to 1850, fenced off common land that villagers had farmed and grazed for generations and turned it into private holdings. Marx later described this fencing-off as one of the founding acts of capitalism: people who had lived from the commons were pushed off the land with nothing left to sell but their labor, and they became the workforce for the factories that followed. The economic historian Karl Polanyi called land, labor and money "fictitious commodities," things never produced to be sold that markets nonetheless began treating as if they were (Polanyi, The Great Transformation).
What made labor sellable was time. Lewis Mumford argued that the key machine of the modern industrial age was not the steam engine but the clock (Mumford, Technics and Civilization). The habit of regular hours began in the medieval monasteries, where bells marked the canonical hours of prayer and gave the day a fixed and repeating order. The mechanical clock, spreading through European towns from the fourteenth century, carried that order out into ordinary life. For Mumford, the clock did something deeper than tell time. It separated time from human events and from the rhythms of nature, turning it into an independent, measurable quantity that could be counted, saved, spent and sold. The historian E. P. Thompson traced what this meant for work (Thompson, "Time, Work-Discipline, and Industrial Capitalism"). Before industrialization, most people worked by the task: the field was harvested when the grain was ripe, the cow milked when it needed milking, and the day's work ended when the job was done. The factory replaced the task with the hour. Bells and clocks set when work began and ended, wages were paid by time, and lateness was fined. Benjamin Franklin's 1748 advice to young tradesmen, "Remember that time is money," became the creed of the age. Time was no longer passed but spent, and once it could be measured in hours, a person's working life could be bought and sold in hours too. Labor became a commodity because time had become one first.
The enclosure of goods followed the same path. Handmade craft gave way to mass-produced commodities, and the worker no longer sold a finished thing but hours of time. Across the Atlantic, the enclosure of labor took its most brutal form. The sugar, cotton and tobacco that fed European markets were produced by enslaved Africans, human beings legally defined as property and the logic of ownership reached towards the body itself.
Meanwhile, England's Statute of Monopolies in 1624 laid the basis for patents, and the Statute of Anne in 1710 created the first copyright law. Ideas and writing, which the first enclosure had placed in the hands of institutions, could now be owned outright as property by individuals and firms and the enclosure of the mind had begun to take a legal form.
The citizen was now defined by property. Ireton's answer at Putney became the rule: voting was usually restricted to those who owned land or paid a certain level of tax, so that those who held a stake in the country had a say in governing it. The French Declaration of the Rights of Man in 1789 described property as an inviolable and sacred right. Citizenship also began to attach to birthplace as well as blood. The principle of jus soli, citizenship by place of birth, took hold in the Americas and was written into the US Constitution by the Fourteenth Amendment in 1868. Yet the body still mattered. Revolutionary France's mass conscription of 1793 created citizen armies, and the soldiers who fought for the nation pressed their claims on it.
From 1850 to 1900, industrial capitalism and new communication tools pulled the era together. The telegraph separated the message from the messenger for the first time, so information could travel faster than any horse or ship. Its early forms were tools of the state. France's optical telegraph network, built in the 1790s, was a government and military system closed to private use. The electric telegraph that followed opened communication to business and the public, but at a price per word. Merchants responded with codebooks that compressed whole sentences into single words, and information itself became something bought and sold by the unit (Gleick, The Information, ch. 5). Telegraph lines linked distant markets so that cotton, grain and stock prices could be set across continents, turning goods into commodities with a single price over vast distances. Railways completed the work the clock had begun. Before the railways, each town kept its own local time by the sun. Trains needed a single schedule, so Britain adopted standard railway time in the 1840s, and North American railroads introduced time zones in 1883. Time was now uniform across whole nations, set by the needs of commerce.
The same networks that concentrated markets also concentrated people. Factories and railways drew workers into cities where they could organize, strike and vote. Britain's Chartists demanded the vote for working men in the 1830s and 40s, reviving Rainsborough's argument two centuries later, and the Reform Acts of 1832, 1867 and 1884 widened the franchise step by step. Across Europe and the Americas, the first modern democratic wave rose because the system needed the many: as taxpayers, as workers, and as soldiers.
The era closes with the first attempt to turn thought itself into machinery. In the 1820s, Charles Babbage designed his Difference Engine to perform calculations that rooms of human "computers" had done by hand, and with Ada Lovelace he imagined an Analytical Engine that could carry out any sequence of operations (Gleick, ch. 4). In his 1832 book On the Economy of Machinery and Manufactures, Babbage showed how dividing work into simple steps allowed each step to be done by cheaper labor or by machines. The person who once made the whole thing now performed one measured part of it. That logic of breaking work down, timing it and recording it would define the next enclosure of labor and data.
The Surveillance Citizen: The Enclosure of Labor and Data (1920 – 2005)
The third enclosure begins where Babbage left off, with work broken into measured parts. In 1911, Frederick Winslow Taylor published The Principles of Scientific Management, which taught managers to stand over workers with a stopwatch, time every motion, and redesign each task down to the second. Two years later, Henry Ford's moving assembly line put that logic into steel. The worker no longer decided how the work was done, because that knowledge now belonged to management, written into instructions, schedules and time studies. The labor historian Harry Braverman later described this as the separation of conception from execution: thinking about the work was taken from the worker and moved into the office, and the worker was left to carry out steps someone else had designed (Braverman, Labor and Monopoly Capital). Lewis Mumford also argued that the factory was modeled on the army, with workers drilled to move as interchangeable parts, and he traced this kind of organization back to the first kingdoms that could assemble thousands of people into a single human machine (Mumford, Technics and Civilization; The Myth of the Machine). The enclosure of labor that began with the clock was now complete: not only the worker's time but the worker's knowledge of their own craft had been captured and owned.
The watching did not stop at the factory gate. When Ford introduced his famous five-dollar day in 1914, workers could qualify for the full wage only if they passed inspection by the company's Sociological Department, whose investigators visited employees' homes to check their finances, their household habits and their family lives. The employer now reached into the private life of the worker. That move, the workplace extending its view into the home, marks the opening of the surveillance citizen.
In 1920, a League of Nations conference in Paris standardized the modern passport, the booklet with photograph and personal details that still exists today. Before the First World War, people could cross much of Europe with little or no paperwork. After it, movement itself required a record issued by the state. The sociologist John Torpey argues that modern states came to control the legitimate means of movement, deciding who could leave, who could enter, and who counted as a member at all (Torpey, The Invention of the Passport). The citizen was becoming a document.
Information technology made the document possible at scale. Herman Hollerith's punch-card tabulating machines, first used for the 1890 US census, turned people into holes in cards that machines could sort and count. His company became part of IBM. The same technology served the welfare state and the police state alike. Social Security numbers, introduced in the United States in 1936, gave every worker a permanent identifying record so that benefits could be paid. In Nazi Germany, census data processed on punch-card machines helped the regime identify Jewish citizens. The record that could protect a person could also mark them.
The deeper change came in how information itself was understood. In 1948, Claude Shannon published his mathematical theory of communication, which defined information as something that could be measured in bits, entirely separate from what it meant (Gleick, The Information, ch. 7). The same year, Norbert Wiener named a new science, cybernetics, from the Greek word for a ship's steersman: the study of control and communication through feedback, in machines and living things alike (Gleick, ch. 8). Together these ideas made it possible to treat any human activity as a signal to be recorded, measured and fed back into systems of control. Where the first enclosure captured meaning within writing, this one stripped meaning away and kept the data.
The citizen of this era was still needed as worker, soldier and now consumer, but was increasingly known to the state and the market as a file. Credit bureaus compiled records of how people paid their debts, and in 1989 the credit score reduced a person's financial life to a single number. Closed-circuit television, first used by the German military in 1942 to watch rocket launches, entered public streets in the 1960s and spread widely through the 1990s. In East Germany, the Stasi built one of the most thorough surveillance systems in history, with files on millions of citizens. It is worth noticing what that system required: tens of thousands of officers and a vast network of informants. Surveillance in this era still depended on people, and so it still had limits. In the West, the scale of watching came to light in the 1970s, when the US Church Committee exposed programs that had spied on civil rights leaders and political groups. The first data privacy laws followed, including the Fair Credit Reporting Act of 1970 and the Privacy Act of 1974. The philosopher Michel Foucault, writing in 1975, described how modern institutions shaped people not by force but by observation: once people know they may be watched, they begin to watch and regulate themselves (Foucault, Discipline and Punish). The surveillance citizen learned to govern their own behavior as a file would be read.
Democracy moved through this era in waves, and communication technology shaped every one of them. Roosevelt's fireside chats brought the president into American homes, while the Nazi regime mass-produced cheap radio sets so that a single voice could reach every household, and the first reverse wave of the 1920s and 30s was partly a radio-age phenomenon. After the Second World War, the second wave rebuilt occupied countries as democracies, extended the vote to women across much of Europe, and through decolonization created dozens of new nations. In the United States, the Voting Rights Act of 1965 finally enforced the vote for Black citizens. The welfare state that grew alongside these democracies depended on records: to provide pensions, health care and unemployment insurance, the state had to know its citizens in detail. A second reverse wave followed in the 1960s and 70s, as Cold War powers backed military coups across Latin America, Africa, Asia and southern Europe. The third wave, beginning in Portugal in 1974 and reaching Eastern Europe in 1989, rode the same tools that enabled surveillance: fax machines, photocopied underground writing, cassette tapes smuggled into Iran, satellite television crossing borders.
The era closes with the internet. Built as a military research network in 1969 and opened to the public through the World Wide Web in the early 1990s, it looked at first like the most open information technology ever made. But the means of watching arrived almost immediately. In 1994, the web browser cookie made it possible for websites to recognize returning visitors, and advertising networks soon used cookies to follow people across many sites and build profiles of their interests. Google, founded in 1998, sits at the hinge. The scholar Shoshana Zuboff argues that in the early 2000s Google discovered that the data people left behind while searching, which she calls behavioral surplus, could be used to predict what they would do next, and that those predictions could be sold (Zuboff, The Age of Surveillance Capitalism). After the September 11 attacks, governments moved in the same direction, expanding surveillance powers and launching programs to gather and combine data on entire populations. The record was no longer only something kept about a person. It was becoming something used to anticipate and steer them. That shift, from recording the citizen to predicting the citizen, would define the next enclosure of knowledge.
The Algorithmic Citizen: The Enclosure of Knowledge (2014 onward)
The fourth enclosure begins, like the others, with an opening. The years between 2005 and 2014 put more information-making power into ordinary hands than any period before. Facebook launched in 2004, YouTube in 2005 and Twitter in 2006. The iPhone arrived in 2007 and the App Store a year later, placing a camera, a microphone, a location tracker and a publishing platform in billions of pockets. Cloud computing made vast processing power available to anyone who could rent it. In 2008, an anonymous author calling themselves Satoshi Nakamoto proposed Bitcoin, a currency meant to run outside the control of banks and states. For a brief moment in the early 2010s, social media looked like a liberation technology, most visibly during the Arab Spring, when protesters used it to organize faster than governments could respond.
However, the enclosure followed quickly. In 2012, two breakthroughs arrived in the same year: a deep learning system called AlexNet showed that neural networks trained on enormous amounts of data could outperform every earlier approach to recognizing images, and CRISPR gave scientists a precise tool for editing genes. In 2013, Edward Snowden revealed that intelligence agencies were collecting phone records and internet communications on a massive scale, often with the cooperation of the same technology companies people trusted with their daily lives. The open network of the early web had become a system for gathering data, and the next decade would show what could be done with it.
What defines this enclosure is aggregation rather than AI on its own. The surveillance citizen was recorded in separate files: a credit report here, a census entry there, a camera on a street corner. The algorithmic citizen is assembled. Data brokers buy and sell records from thousands of sources, combining purchases, locations, browsing histories and public records into a single profile. Phones, cars, smart speakers, doorbells and wearable devices pour data from the physical world into the same pools. AI systems manage these pools and turn them into decisions about who gets a loan, what someone pays for insurance, whose job application is read, and who counts as a threat. These profiles are the descendants of the user profiles early Google built to sell advertising, but they now reach far beyond advertising into the decisions that shape a person's rights and opportunities. Shoshana Zuboff's insight about the previous era has come to full scale: the data a person produces is used not only to describe them but to predict and steer what they will do (Zuboff, The Age of Surveillance Capitalism). James C. Scott's early grain states wanted their people to be legible, countable and taxable. The algorithmic state and market can now read people more completely than any ruler of the past could have imagined (Scott, Seeing Like a State).
The deepest part of this enclosure is the capture of knowledge itself, and it brings history full circle. The first enclosure moved memory out of people and into written objects that institutions could own. The fourth gathers the written record of humanity, its books, articles, encyclopedias, forums and conversations, into large language models owned by a handful of companies. The legal scholar James Boyle warned in 2003 of a "second enclosure movement," in which the expansion of intellectual property would fence off the commons of ideas the way the enclosure acts fenced off common land (Boyle, "The Second Enclosure Movement"). AI carries that enclosure a step further. Knowledge that was written by millions of people and held in common is absorbed into systems that answer questions on their behalf. Authors, artists and publishers have taken AI companies to court over how their work was used, and those fights are about who owns the shared record of human thought. Plato's old objection to writing was that a text cannot answer back. Now the text answers back, fluently and on any subject. But the answers come from a system that someone else owns, trains and controls, and the frame of thought that once belonged to scribes and clergy now belongs to whoever builds the model.
The body has been enclosed again as well. Consumer genetic testing invited millions of people to send their DNA to private companies, and when 23andMe filed for bankruptcy in 2025, the fate of its customers' genetic data became part of the sale. The biological citizen was defined by blood; the algorithmic citizen's blood has become data. Fitness trackers, sleep monitors and health apps record heart rates, menstrual cycles and moods. Brain-computer interfaces have begun moving from laboratories into human trials. Foucault argued that the surveillance citizen learned to regulate their own behavior under the possibility of being watched. The algorithmic citizen goes further and volunteers the data, managing daily life through apps that measure, score and nudge. Self-tracking that began as self-care becomes another stream feeding the pool.
The fights over control have been fought over encryption and money. When Apple refused in 2016 to help the FBI unlock a suspect's iPhone, it reopened an argument that had simmered since the 1990s over whether people may keep communications that governments cannot read. End-to-end encryption spread to messaging apps used by billions. Cryptocurrency tried to take money itself outside the control of banks and states, Polanyi's third fictitious commodity pulled briefly back toward the commons. Within fifteen years, it too was largely absorbed into the system it set out to escape, traded through regulated funds and major financial institutions.
Physical AI then completes the shift, carrying the enclosure of knowledge from ideas into the making of things. Robots, autonomous vehicles and drones extend AI from processing information to acting in the world. While, the first wave of large AI models absorbed the written record thru books, articles, encyclopedias and conversations – physical AI reaches for the knowledge of how things are made thru capturing designs, engineering methods, manufacturing processes, and the results of experiments that show what actually works in the physical world.
Jeff Bezos's Prometheus provides a preview of where this is all heading. The New York Times reported in November 2025 that Bezos would serve as co-founder and co-CEO of the company, which launched with $6.2 billion in funding, co-led with Vik Bajaj, who worked on the self-driving car project that became Waymo and co-founded the life sciences lab Verily. Its stated aim is AI that learns from the physical world rather than only processing digital information, applied to engineering and manufacturing in areas such as vehicles and space technology. Bezos has described it as building an "artificial general engineer," a modern descendant of computer-aided design focused on designing physical objects. The plan reaches beyond software. According to the Financial Times, Prometheus has sought tens of billions more to form a holding company that would acquire industrial firms facing disruption from AI, and the company has since raised a further $12 billion. The model is not only to build tools for engineers but to own the companies whose designs, processes and engineering knowledge those tools learn from. The name fits more than its founders may have intended. In Greek myth, Prometheus stole fire from the gods and gave it to humanity. Fire is where this history began, with cooking and the gathering of people into larger groups. The new Prometheus gathers the fire of making into a single private company.
Knowledge of making has always been the hardest kind to enclose, because so much of it lives in people rather than on paper. The philosopher Michael Polanyi, brother of the economist Karl Polanyi, observed that we know more than we can tell: a skilled maker carries knowledge in their hands and judgment that no manual fully captures (Polanyi, The Tacit Dimension). That tacit knowledge kept craft, engineering and invention in human hands even as other things were enclosed. Taylor took the worker's craft knowledge and moved it into management's instructions, which Braverman called the separation of conception from execution. Physical AI goes further and moves conception itself, the design, the engineering judgment and the creative act, into systems trained on every design and experiment their owners can acquire. Creation itself can now be automated, and what can be automated can be owned.
The same process is reaching the knowledge of life. In 2020, DeepMind's AlphaFold predicted the three-dimensional structures of proteins, the molecules that carry out nearly every process in living things, with accuracy that had eluded scientists for fifty years; its creators shared the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. AI systems now design new proteins and drug candidates. The knowledge of how life builds itself and the knowledge of how people build the world are both being gathered, modeled and held. This is the most fundamental enclosure of knowledge in the sequence. The first enclosure captured what people knew. This one captures how things, and life itself, come to be.
This is where the question that runs through this whole history becomes most urgent. The Stasi needed tens of thousands of officers and informants to watch its population, and that need was itself a limit. Dario Amodei has warned that autocracies have always been restrained by their dependence on people to carry out their orders, and that AI could remove that restraint (Amodei, "The Adolescence of Technology"). The same logic applies to the economy. Luke Drago and Rudolf Laine argue that if powerful institutions no longer need people's labor, they lose their incentive to invest in those people, the way oil-rich states often neglect their citizens because their wealth comes from the ground (Drago and Laine, "The Intelligence Curse"). When even design, engineering and invention no longer require the many, the last form of necessity that gave ordinary people leverage begins to disappear. For the first time in this history, a technology may allow the system to function, and even to create, without needing the many at all.
Democracy in this era has moved backward. Organizations such as Freedom House and V-Dem have recorded more countries losing democratic ground than gaining it each year since the mid-2000s, which lines up closely with the rise of platform media and data aggregation. The early promise of social media gave way to disinformation, algorithmic polarization and the concentration of public attention in a few platforms. Responses have come, as they did in the previous era. The European Union's General Data Protection Regulation in 2018 gave people new rights over their personal data, and its AI Act of 2024 began setting rules for how AI systems may be used. Whether such laws can give citizens real power to enforce their rights, and whether anyone can be held accountable when an algorithm crosses a boundary, remain open questions.
The algorithmic citizen is defined by the data profile. Decisions that shape a person's rights, opportunities and safety are increasingly made by systems the person cannot see, question or appeal, much like the Domesday Book, whose record could not be argued with. The citizen still generates what the system needs: data, attention, behavior to be predicted. But the system may no longer need the citizen to generate it. 
Conclusion
This history began with fire. Cooking gathered people into larger groups, but as Lucassen reminds us, technology did not build the polis on its own; it waited for changes in work, labor and surplus to force people to reorganize. The same holds across every era traced here. Technology and democracy have never moved separately. Each new technology has changed who is necessary, and each change in who is necessary has changed who holds power.
Four times, the same pattern has repeated. A new technology opens something to the many. The alphabet let ordinary Athenians read their laws. The printing press broke the church's and the court's hold on the written word. Broadcast, telecommunication and the early internet carried voices across borders that no censor could fully close. Social media and the smartphone put the power to publish in billions of hands. Each opening spread power out, and democracy rose with it. Then an enclosure followed. Writing settled into the hands of scribes and clergy. Common land was fenced, time was sold by the hour, and ideas became property. Work was timed, people were filed, and data was harvested. Knowledge, and now the knowledge of making itself, is being gathered into systems owned by a few. Each enclosure concentrated power, and democracy fell back.
With each enclosure, the citizen changed. The biological citizen was defined by the body: by birth, and by the ability to fight and row. The natural rights citizen was defined by property: by land owned and taxes paid. The surveillance citizen was defined by the record: the passport, the census card, the credit file. The algorithmic citizen is defined by the data profile, assembled from every purchase, movement, search and heartbeat. At every stage, the citizen was valued for what the system needed from them, and democracy was strongest when the system needed the many. Kings who needed soldiers and taxpayers had to bargain with them. Factory owners who needed workers had to face their unions. States that needed citizens' cooperation to govern had to answer to them.
Each enclosure also provoked resistance. Karl Polanyi called this the double movement: whenever markets push to turn more of life into commodities, society pushes back to protect itself (Polanyi, The Great Transformation). The Levellers argued at Putney that the poorest had as much right to a voice as the greatest. The Chartists, the labor unions and the suffrage movements forced the vote open step by step. Factory laws limited the working day. Privacy laws answered the Church Committee's revelations, and the GDPR and the AI Act answer the age of aggregation. The enclosures have never been total, because people have never stopped claiming back what was taken.
What makes this moment different is the question that runs through the whole history: whether the system still needs the many. Every earlier enclosure captured something people had, but it still depended on people to produce it. Writing needed scribes. The factory needed workers. The Stasi needed informants. Even surveillance capitalism needed users to generate data. The algorithmic enclosure is the first in which the system may be able to function, and even to create, without needing most people at all. If design, engineering, invention and enforcement can all be automated, the last source of leverage ordinary people have held since the hoplites, their necessity, begins to disappear. This is the endpoint Marx warned about, carried further than he could have imagined. Labor did not merely become a commodity. The citizen risks dissolving into the data the system draws from them, valued for what can be extracted and no longer needed as a person.
That outcome is not settled. Technology by itself is neither democratic nor authoritarian. Mumford distinguished between authoritarian technics, large and centrally controlled, and democratic technics, small in scale, centered on the person, and widely held. The same tools that enclose can also open, as the printing press, the fax machine and the early internet each showed. Businesses that have lasted have often done so by making more people necessary rather than fewer, building ecosystems that others could build their lives on. Whether AI follows that path or the path of enclosure depends on choices about who owns these systems, who governs them, who can enforce their rights against them, and who is held accountable when they cross a boundary.
Polybius believed every system carries the conditions that can undo it. Democracy has fallen many times and risen again, each time because enough people kept insisting that power be spread out and shared. In the myth, Prometheus stole fire from the gods and gave it to humanity. The question now is whether the new fire will be given or kept, whether this technology will make the many necessary again or fence them out of the future they helped build.
